Patch 6006: cellFsRead, Write, Lseek and Fstat hold the FILE*'s own stdio lock across their steps
(FS_STREAM_LOCK=0 off).

Several guest threads share one fd. Fstat seeks to the end and back, Lseek does tell/seek/tell and Read does tell then
read, with nothing held across the steps, so another thread's step could land in between: a read at the wrong offset or
at EOF, a short read a loader retries. Only cellFsAioRead held the lock. Found in another title's port (a freeze while
streaming a map); not seen in J-Stars, same code. Upstream candidate.

Tested: in the same build as 1004 (bench\exp\2026-11-07_2235-v15on1, 2317-v15on2): boots, demo and Free Battle run.

diff --git a/runtime/ppu/ppu_fs.cpp b/runtime/ppu/ppu_fs.cpp
index 23544682..dba84d4a 100644
--- a/runtime/ppu/ppu_fs.cpp
+++ b/runtime/ppu/ppu_fs.cpp
@@ -77,6 +77,30 @@ static int FS_STAT64(const char* p, fs_stat64_t* s)
 typedef struct stat fs_stat64_t;
 #endif
 
+/* FS_STREAM_LOCK (default on, FS_STREAM_LOCK=0 off; patch 6006): several guest threads share one fd. Fstat (seek
+ * to the end and back), Lseek (tell, seek, tell) and Read (tell, then read) took several steps on the shared FILE*
+ * without holding anything across them, so another thread's step could land in between: a read at the wrong offset
+ * or at EOF, a short read a loader retries forever. Each of them now holds the stream's own lock (recursive, per
+ * FILE*; stdio's calls take the same one, and so does cellFsAioRead) across its steps. Found by another v19 port. */
+#ifdef _WIN32
+#  define FS_LOCKF(f)   _lock_file(f)
+#  define FS_UNLOCKF(f) _unlock_file(f)
+#else
+#  define FS_LOCKF(f)   flockfile(f)
+#  define FS_UNLOCKF(f) funlockfile(f)
+#endif
+static int fs_stream_lock_on(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) { const char* e = getenv("FS_STREAM_LOCK"); on = (e && *e == '0') ? 0 : 1; }
+    return on;
+}
+struct FsStreamGuard {
+    FILE* f;
+    explicit FsStreamGuard(FILE* fp) : f(fs_stream_lock_on() ? fp : nullptr) { if (f) FS_LOCKF(f); }
+    ~FsStreamGuard() { if (f) FS_UNLOCKF(f); }
+};
+
